KLEE on OpenBSD
================

POSIX runtime and external libraries
------------------------------------

KLEE's support for running real (non-closed) programs is built on top of
uClibc, which is Linux-specific and cannot be built on OpenBSD.  This
package is therefore configured with -DENABLE_POSIX_RUNTIME=OFF and ships
only the freestanding, intrinsic, klee-libc, fortify and UBSan runtime
libraries.

As a result KLEE can only execute closed programs, i.e. programs that do
not call into an external C library.  For anything else you need to
provide a bitcode version of the C library yourself.

Memory usage
------------

By default KLEE uses the deterministic allocator (KDAlloc), which reserves
large amounts of virtual address space (for example 1 TiB for the heap).
On OpenBSD `mmap(2)` may refuse such requests depending on `kern.maxdsiz`
and the amount of swap.  If KLEE aborts with a message about the
"Deterministic allocator" not being able to allocate a mapping, reduce the
corresponding sizes, for example:

	$ klee -kdalloc-heap-size=4 -kdalloc-stack-size=4 \
	    -kdalloc-globals-size=2 -kdalloc-constants-size=2 ...

Alternatively, disable the deterministic allocator with `-no-kdalloc`.
